#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179724 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179586 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "189f51d6-494f-515a-bf77-59496421b093" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Line Integral Along a Segment in the Cartesian Plane"}] :question/id #uuid "b7d08596-fbe4-5601-a58e-dac90715f7e7" :question/math-academy-id "e-9486" :question/problem "Find a definite integral that is equivalent to the line integral of the function $f(x,y) = x+\\sin{y},$ traversed along the line segment from $(1,0)$ to $(1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s  =  \\int\\limits_{t_a}^{t_b}  f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\| \\, \\textrm{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(1,0)$ to $(1,1).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $\\mathbf{a}=\\mathbf{i}$ and $\\mathbf{b}=\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =i+t[(i+j)-i] \\\\  & =i+t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0 \\le t \\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=1$ and $y=t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =0\\,i+j \\\\  & =j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y) = x+\\sin{y}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "189f51d6-494f-515a-bf77-59496421b093" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050289}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542352 :question-generator/id #uuid "ab3697ad-3fd9-5d1a-b478-d2622f1f3912" :question-generator/script "generate/scratch/17592186179724.py"} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179981 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "189f51d6-494f-515a-bf77-59496421b093" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Line Integral Along a Segment in the Cartesian Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c16fe8e-df28-5acf-80d4-30daf328aacf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{10}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{2}-t+2)\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253906} {:answer/id #uuid "5aca3d77-fbac-59a8-a8e8-f141fffb748d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{2}+t-2)\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254472} {:answer/id #uuid "6641d652-1c79-5594-8a40-67438b550538" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{10}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(9{t}^{2}+t-2)\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254589} {:answer/id #uuid "b2a3d234-e957-5e8d-85be-bdebd0026797" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10{\\int }_{0}^{1}(9{t}^{2}+t-2)\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255242} {:answer/id #uuid "edb11fd3-821e-5d36-891b-095d0de53681" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{2}-t+2)\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255748}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6641d652-1c79-5594-8a40-67438b550538" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{10}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(9{t}^{2}+t-2)\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254589} :answer-field/id #uuid "761e5ab1-caf4-5260-859c-054aab437559" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256139}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "09a46495-b2d7-513a-938f-504451202710" :question/math-academy-id "q-123127" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-y,$ traversed along the line segment from $(0,2)$ to $(3,1)?$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(0,2)$ to $(3,1).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=2\\,j$ and $b=3\\,i+j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =2\\,j+t[(3\\,i+j)-2\\,j] \\\\  & =2\\,j+t(3\\,i-j) \\\\  & =3t\\,i+(2-t)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=3t$ and $y=2-t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2-t)j \\\\  & =3\\,i-j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(3t)^{2}-(2-t) \\\\  & =9{t}^{2}+t-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(9{t}^{2}+t-2)\\cdot \\sqrt{10}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(9{t}^{2}+t-2)\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179986 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "189f51d6-494f-515a-bf77-59496421b093" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Line Integral Along a Segment in the Cartesian Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "711177e4-868e-5ac6-a2b1-961ab8f94f30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2-t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257775} {:answer/id #uuid "8860493e-bab1-52a0-9bf9-eb7d11745d38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3-2t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257977} {:answer/id #uuid "b78bc20f-293d-5acf-9926-a6d8bb185d88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3-2t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258386} {:answer/id #uuid "db0fb6b5-5dcc-57a4-a8f1-684f9c0bb985" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4-t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258695} {:answer/id #uuid "f570bf13-b1d2-5f54-853f-bb075d272c17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4-t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258924}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "db0fb6b5-5dcc-57a4-a8f1-684f9c0bb985" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4-t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258695} :answer-field/id #uuid "d19b03b6-7a81-55e6-a56b-0d4882ea6475" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259400}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9fb44c65-3cac-5280-b910-d92f6181f8ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-123269" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y),$ traversed along the line segment from $(1,3)$ to $(0,3)?$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(1,3)$ to $(0,3).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=i+3\\,j$ and $b=3\\,j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =(i+3\\,j)+t[3\\,j-(i+3\\,j)] \\\\  & =(i+3\\,j)-t\\,i \\\\  & =(1-t)\\,i+3\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=1-t$ and $y=3$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3)j \\\\  & =-i \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((1-t)+3) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4-t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4-t)\\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4-t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259501 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "189f51d6-494f-515a-bf77-59496421b093" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Line Integral Along a Segment in the Cartesian Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "536e63b2-339e-55fb-9ef6-16302fa8d80b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1))\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257495} {:answer/id #uuid "562e0f5a-9f51-526c-b575-21a080e41feb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{\\int }_{0}^{1}(t-1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257523} {:answer/id #uuid "67f3bbaf-7696-53c7-a073-517185ea4bb9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(4t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257700} {:answer/id #uuid "714228f8-6684-5ac7-831f-a8ce0050c5dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1))\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257778} {:answer/id #uuid "83d7a139-b408-5fd1-8726-70337664b912" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(t-1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257946}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "536e63b2-339e-55fb-9ef6-16302fa8d80b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1))\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257495} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f23d5c3-adfc-5e83-a5b1-c055960487eb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259115}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b87e3ea6-f1f9-5cac-b915-0751187e2e15" :question/math-academy-id "q-123268" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=1+x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y,$ traversed along the line segment from $(-1,1)$ to $(1,2)?$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(-1,1)$ to $(1,2).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=-i+j$ and $b=i+2\\,j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =(-i+j)+t[(i+2\\,j)-(-i+j)] \\\\  & =(-i+j)+t(2\\,i+j) \\\\  & =(2t-1)\\,i+(t+1)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=2t-1$ and $y=t+1$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t-1)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+1)j \\\\  & =2\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=1+x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =1+(2t-1)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1) \\\\  & =2t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1))\\cdot \\sqrt{5}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1))\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259503 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "189f51d6-494f-515a-bf77-59496421b093" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Line Integral Along a Segment in the Cartesian Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e37f599-d9fb-59d9-86fe-bf8eaa2a4cda" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(12t-5)\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256920} {:answer/id #uuid "1975ac33-0269-5ed1-88f8-8afee74b72b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "29{\\int }_{0}^{1}(10t-3)\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257001} {:answer/id #uuid "2f82862b-d6d3-54c7-bc2b-fdf11833df09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{29}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(8t+7)\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257200} {:answer/id #uuid "33c69e8d-5ac9-5a07-ab6f-0a29d6e3e45b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{29}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(12t-5)\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257240} {:answer/id #uuid "fbcdea73-c430-5bfe-8af1-0bac80bdcafe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(8t+7)\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258984}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "33c69e8d-5ac9-5a07-ab6f-0a29d6e3e45b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{29}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(12t-5)\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257240} :answer-field/id #uuid "05a11e2f-b331-55cb-99aa-ab3cf37cfaf5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259043}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "12a82c22-3ce2-5dee-95e7-fff601263913" :question/math-academy-id "q-123279" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=2x+y,$ traversed along the line segment from $(-3,1)$ to $(2,3)?$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(-3,1)$ to $(2,3).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=-3\\,i+j$ and $b=2\\,i+3\\,j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =(-3\\,i+j)+t[(2\\,i+3\\,j)-(-3\\,i+j)] \\\\  & =(-3\\,i+j)+t(5\\,i+2\\,j) \\\\  & =(5t-3)\\,i+(1+2t)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=5t-3$ and $y=1+2t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5t-3)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+2t)j \\\\  & =5\\,i+2\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{5}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{29}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=2x+y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =2(5t-3)+(1+2t) \\\\  & =12t-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(12t-5)\\cdot \\sqrt{29}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{29}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(12t-5)\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387367 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "189f51d6-494f-515a-bf77-59496421b093" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Line Integral Along a Segment in the Cartesian Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c9653f49-1a01-5acd-8ce6-f3cd8095fdbc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{7t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387369} {:answer/id #uuid "2d3322db-d223-55b3-b7dc-f9fd758349fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387370} {:answer/id #uuid "e3c61a6c-a91d-50a4-a701-bf4e530a3094" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387371} {:answer/id #uuid "d4be85c4-9c1a-5af1-b31a-957bb0b77474" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387372} {:answer/id #uuid "95f050db-1db6-5212-8a8b-3de82028eac7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{7t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387373}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d4be85c4-9c1a-5af1-b31a-957bb0b77474" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387372} :answer-field/id #uuid "e77fad7f-b6e3-5827-9df5-363a99a7be9d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387368}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b2a142a9-d042-56b2-ba86-b30a7fb0dfd2" :question/math-academy-id "q-123283" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral of the function $f(x,y)={e}^{x+y},$ traversed along the line segment from $(0,0)$ to $(-3,4)?$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(0,0)$ to $(-3,4).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=0$ and $b=-3\\,i+4\\,j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =0+t\\,(b-0) \\\\  & =t(b) \\\\  & =t(-3\\,i+4\\,j) \\\\  & =-3t\\,i+4t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=-3t$ and $y=4t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-3t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t)j \\\\  & =-3\\,i+4\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)={e}^{x+y}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & ={e}^{-3t+4t} \\\\  & ={e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{t}\\cdot 5\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =5{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542282 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "189f51d6-494f-515a-bf77-59496421b093" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Line Integral Along a Segment in the Cartesian Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "87e7020a-b4c1-5c1e-970a-f3080b2f18a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{25}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-5t+2)\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542312} {:answer/id #uuid "a21fd961-41aa-514a-84a6-bd53640d329b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-5t+2)\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542319} {:answer/id #uuid "dfb7ea70-ab48-5b5f-a35f-b10fa2c57fea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-3t+2)\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542339} {:answer/id #uuid "e07e6d25-a555-5fcd-a7a4-bc176e9d0fa6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-2t+2)\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542341} {:answer/id #uuid "e433ebda-2236-542b-93b3-4804ba472bef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-5t+2)\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542343}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a21fd961-41aa-514a-84a6-bd53640d329b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-5t+2)\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542319} :answer-field/id #uuid "08cce96c-e9ed-58e4-b741-d8a8f993b454" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542285}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "02bf06bd-a114-5709-91cd-398487459c7f" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=2x+y,$ traversed along the line segment from $(-1,4)$ to $(- \\frac{5}{2},2)?$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(-1,4)$ to $(- \\frac{5}{2},2).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=-i+4\\,j$ and $b=-\\frac{5}{2}\\,i+2\\,j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =(-i+4\\,j)+t[(-\\frac{5}{2}\\,i+2\\,j)-(-i+4\\,j)] \\\\  & =(-i+4\\,j)+t(-\\frac{3}{2}\\,i-2\\,j) \\\\  & =(-\\frac{3}{2}t-1)\\,i+(4-2t)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=-\\frac{3}{2}t-1$ and $y=4-2t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\frac{3}{2}t-1)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4-2t)j \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\,i-2\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(- \\frac{3}{2})^{2}+(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=2x+y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =2(-\\frac{3}{2}t-1)+(4-2t) \\\\  & =-5t+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-5t+2)\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-5t+2)\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542291 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "189f51d6-494f-515a-bf77-59496421b093" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Line Integral Along a Segment in the Cartesian Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "035fa252-c497-5441-a7a9-995b1b999642" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{41}}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(0-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-2))\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542283} {:answer/id #uuid "1931c9f8-d41a-5ef1-b13b-ee9bd8a5e925" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{41}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{5}{2}t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-2))\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542289} {:answer/id #uuid "940e3279-20b0-5c97-a20b-20bc1ccb29af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{41}}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{5}{2}t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2)\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542313} {:answer/id #uuid "c5abffee-6393-58b4-acdb-d0f5a1c85331" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{5}{2}t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-2))\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542331} {:answer/id #uuid "dcb0947a-9d73-5217-8106-27e3a8efa0a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{41}}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{5}{2}t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-2))\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542338}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dcb0947a-9d73-5217-8106-27e3a8efa0a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{41}}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{5}{2}t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-2))\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542338} :answer-field/id #uuid "5fbe6447-d7b2-5aa3-832a-868998a34289" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542304}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2fca5dad-31ea-55b7-94df-b638c15ad9f2" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=1+x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y,$ traversed along the line segment from $(-1,2)$ to $(\\frac{3}{2},0)?$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(-1,2)$ to $(\\frac{3}{2},0).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=-i+2\\,j$ and $b=\\frac{3}{2}\\,i.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =(-i+2\\,j)+t[\\frac{3}{2}\\,i-(-i+2\\,j)] \\\\  & =(-i+2\\,j)+t(\\frac{5}{2}\\,i-2\\,j) \\\\  & =(\\frac{5}{2}t-1)\\,i+(-2t+2)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=\\frac{5}{2}t-1$ and $y=-2t+2$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{5}{2}t-1)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-2t+2)j \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\,i-2\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\frac{5}{2}}^{2}+(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{41}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=1+x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =1+(\\frac{5}{2}t-1)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-2t+2) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{5}{2}t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-2))\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{41}}{2}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{41}}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{5}{2}t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-2))\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542298 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "189f51d6-494f-515a-bf77-59496421b093" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Line Integral Along a Segment in the Cartesian Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "473e8524-7399-51a9-981f-c0aa4668dd93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{577}}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{1}{6}t+\\frac{25}{2})\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542297} {:answer/id #uuid "c04afd16-987b-5ec2-af26-9f9b902a2206" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{577}}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(16{t}^{2}-\\frac{143}{6}t+\\frac{25}{2})\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542328} {:answer/id #uuid "d1d23fa3-0d57-5274-baa4-eef4412f5a91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{577}{36}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(16{t}^{2}-\\frac{143}{6}t+\\frac{25}{2})\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542333} {:answer/id #uuid "d58a6523-8175-5758-9a7a-5450d554c95c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}(16{t}^{2}-\\frac{143}{6}t+\\frac{25}{2})\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542337} {:answer/id #uuid "f6ab0414-6ef1-5f05-b520-6b9c768d227c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{577}}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(16{t}^{2}-24t+\\frac{25}{2})\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542350}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c04afd16-987b-5ec2-af26-9f9b902a2206" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{577}}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(16{t}^{2}-\\frac{143}{6}t+\\frac{25}{2})\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542328} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d4fa989-2ac8-5a30-824a-30041569e8ff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542294}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4b732e53-9da1-5d2f-ab3b-dfb9b5fc9cae" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-y,$ traversed along the line segment from $(3,- \\frac{7}{2})$ to $(-1,- \\frac{11}{3})?$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(3,- \\frac{7}{2})$ to $(-1,- \\frac{11}{3}).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=3\\,i-\\frac{7}{2}\\,j$ and $b=-i-\\frac{11}{3}\\,j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =(3\\,i-\\frac{7}{2}\\,j)+t[(-i-\\frac{11}{3}\\,j)-(3\\,i-\\frac{7}{2}\\,j)] \\\\  & =(3\\,i-\\frac{7}{2}\\,j)+t(-4\\,i-\\frac{1}{6}\\,j) \\\\  & =(-4t+3)\\,i+(-\\frac{7}{2}-\\frac{1}{6}t)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=-4t+3$ and $y=-\\frac{7}{2}-\\frac{1}{6}t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-4t+3)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\frac{7}{2}-\\frac{1}{6}t)j \\\\  & =-4\\,i-\\frac{1}{6}\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-4)^{2}+(- \\frac{1}{6})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{577}}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(-4t+3)^{2}-(-\\frac{7}{2}-\\frac{1}{6}t) \\\\  & =16{t}^{2}-\\frac{143}{6}t+\\frac{25}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(16{t}^{2}-\\frac{143}{6}t+\\frac{25}{2})\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{577}}{6}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{577}}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(16{t}^{2}-\\frac{143}{6}t+\\frac{25}{2})\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542305 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "189f51d6-494f-515a-bf77-59496421b093" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Line Integral Along a Segment in the Cartesian Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b20b7f03-488d-57c7-b9f2-d236742a9961" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{277}}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-3t+\\frac{7}{3})\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542324} {:answer/id #uuid "b77786f1-adbc-5af1-8f60-843037465c66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{277}}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-\\frac{7}{3}t+\\frac{7}{3})\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542326} {:answer/id #uuid "cc8d6535-5549-51c3-b2b4-d17b02464990" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{277}}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-\\frac{16}{3}t+\\frac{7}{3})\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542332} {:answer/id #uuid "f5361eea-4400-528a-850e-0ba9c82a1977" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-\\frac{16}{3}t+\\frac{7}{3})\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542347} {:answer/id #uuid "fd1096ed-014c-57c2-ab1e-36b976f89bba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{277}{36}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-\\frac{16}{3}t+\\frac{7}{3})\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542351}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cc8d6535-5549-51c3-b2b4-d17b02464990" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{277}}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-\\frac{16}{3}t+\\frac{7}{3})\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542332} :answer-field/id #uuid "03e14137-0d58-55a7-bd84-61bca100a01f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542284}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "621c7226-fc49-5cd6-ab81-d4bf845859cb" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=2x+y,$ traversed along the line segment from $(-1,\\frac{13}{3})$ to $(- \\frac{5}{2},2)?$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(-1,\\frac{13}{3})$ to $(- \\frac{5}{2},2).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=-i+\\frac{13}{3}\\,j$ and $b=-\\frac{5}{2}\\,i+2\\,j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =(-i+\\frac{13}{3}\\,j)+t[(-\\frac{5}{2}\\,i+2\\,j)-(-i+\\frac{13}{3}\\,j)] \\\\  & =(-i+\\frac{13}{3}\\,j)+t(-\\frac{3}{2}\\,i-\\frac{7}{3}\\,j) \\\\  & =(-\\frac{3}{2}t-1)\\,i+(\\frac{13}{3}-\\frac{7}{3}t)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=-\\frac{3}{2}t-1$ and $y=\\frac{13}{3}-\\frac{7}{3}t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\frac{3}{2}t-1)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{13}{3}-\\frac{7}{3}t)j \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\,i-\\frac{7}{3}\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(- \\frac{3}{2})^{2}+(- \\frac{7}{3})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{277}}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=2x+y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =2(-\\frac{3}{2}t-1)+(\\frac{13}{3}-\\frac{7}{3}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{16}{3}t+\\frac{7}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-\\frac{16}{3}t+\\frac{7}{3})\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{277}}{6}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{277}}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-\\frac{16}{3}t+\\frac{7}{3})\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542320 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "189f51d6-494f-515a-bf77-59496421b093" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Line Integral Along a Segment in the Cartesian Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17c87f5e-5e1e-533e-a685-95f4e3d70e53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4+\\frac{23}{6}t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542288} {:answer/id #uuid "40fc5e25-69f2-53fc-bfbb-ac186ba68379" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4+\\frac{4}{3}t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542295} {:answer/id #uuid "c42403a1-9e8a-5de5-882b-c347f626e69b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4+\\frac{5}{2}t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542329} {:answer/id #uuid "e3968766-f69b-5abb-9c51-38782ded432b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{289}{36}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4+\\frac{23}{6}t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542342} {:answer/id #uuid "e4b91717-1749-5c7a-be22-16af96b4ab7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4+\\frac{23}{6}t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542344}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e4b91717-1749-5c7a-be22-16af96b4ab7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4+\\frac{23}{6}t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542344} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f19d3c7-bdba-598e-85e9-a9f2767c5565" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542311}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a221b842-bd46-5723-b036-3a6f86665976" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y),$ traversed along the line segment from $(1,3)$ to $(\\frac{7}{2},\\frac{13}{3})?$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(1,3)$ to $(\\frac{7}{2},\\frac{13}{3}).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=i+3\\,j$ and $b=\\frac{7}{2}\\,i+\\frac{13}{3}\\,j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =(i+3\\,j)+t[(\\frac{7}{2}\\,i+\\frac{13}{3}\\,j)-(i+3\\,j)] \\\\  & =(i+3\\,j)+t(\\frac{5}{2}\\,i+\\frac{4}{3}\\,j) \\\\  & =(1+\\frac{5}{2}t)\\,i+(3+\\frac{4}{3}t)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=1+\\frac{5}{2}t$ and $y=3+\\frac{4}{3}t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+\\frac{5}{2}t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3+\\frac{4}{3}t)j \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\,i+\\frac{4}{3}\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\frac{5}{2}}^{2}+{\\frac{4}{3}}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{17}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((1+\\frac{5}{2}t)+3+\\frac{4}{3}t) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4+\\frac{23}{6}t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4+\\frac{23}{6}t)\\cdot \\frac{17}{6}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{17}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4+\\frac{23}{6}t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542325 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "189f51d6-494f-515a-bf77-59496421b093" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Line Integral Along a Segment in the Cartesian Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1474a45f-3cb5-5c5c-82a7-b35bad7ae911" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{61}}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-\\frac{5}{2}t+\\frac{15}{2})\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542287} {:answer/id #uuid "30335a9e-1e6a-5240-a835-635bf13e5660" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{61}}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(9{t}^{2}-\\frac{29}{2}t+\\frac{15}{2})\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542292} {:answer/id #uuid "63e0ed3c-b3f8-5a81-b935-2f28c7723aa0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{61}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(9{t}^{2}-\\frac{29}{2}t+\\frac{15}{2})\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542307} {:answer/id #uuid "ae88aef5-06bc-5eb8-bf58-ee6e0e624524" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{61}}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(9{t}^{2}-12t+\\frac{15}{2})\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542323} {:answer/id #uuid "f3aa4113-237e-5e1a-ac67-eb9f38bf7ad3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}(9{t}^{2}-\\frac{29}{2}t+\\frac{15}{2})\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542346}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "30335a9e-1e6a-5240-a835-635bf13e5660" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{61}}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(9{t}^{2}-\\frac{29}{2}t+\\frac{15}{2})\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542292} :answer-field/id #uuid "62aaa0dc-bb9c-53ef-9689-89144abe766e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542306}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b2a5ec51-bad1-5473-9954-53b213963bd6" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-y,$ traversed along the line segment from $(-2,- \\frac{7}{2})$ to $(1,-1)?$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(-2,- \\frac{7}{2})$ to $(1,-1).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=-2\\,i-\\frac{7}{2}\\,j$ and $b=i-j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =(-2\\,i-\\frac{7}{2}\\,j)+t[(i-j)-(-2\\,i-\\frac{7}{2}\\,j)] \\\\  & =(-2\\,i-\\frac{7}{2}\\,j)+t(3\\,i+\\frac{5}{2}\\,j) \\\\  & =(3t-2)\\,i+(-\\frac{7}{2}+\\frac{5}{2}t)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=3t-2$ and $y=-\\frac{7}{2}+\\frac{5}{2}t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t-2)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\frac{7}{2}+\\frac{5}{2}t)j \\\\  & =3\\,i+\\frac{5}{2}\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{\\frac{5}{2}}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{61}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(3t-2)^{2}-(-\\frac{7}{2}+\\frac{5}{2}t) \\\\  & =9{t}^{2}-\\frac{29}{2}t+\\frac{15}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(9{t}^{2}-\\frac{29}{2}t+\\frac{15}{2})\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{61}}{2}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{61}}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(9{t}^{2}-\\frac{29}{2}t+\\frac{15}{2})\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542327 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "189f51d6-494f-515a-bf77-59496421b093" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Line Integral Along a Segment in the Cartesian Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5582abdc-4614-5e71-964a-2d19582d849c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{29}}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4+t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542300} {:answer/id #uuid "6f7b5275-0ea4-503c-a862-6c7508804d3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4+\\frac{7}{2}t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542309} {:answer/id #uuid "70d93491-99f0-5bd9-b153-73c0784e2eeb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{29}}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4+\\frac{5}{2}t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542310} {:answer/id #uuid "9acb3c73-570d-5433-98be-243f021d0f47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{29}}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4+\\frac{7}{2}t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542317} {:answer/id #uuid "9cad3f1b-7cf7-5209-ab65-767609942832" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{29}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4+\\frac{7}{2}t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542318}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9acb3c73-570d-5433-98be-243f021d0f47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{29}}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4+\\frac{7}{2}t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542317} :answer-field/id #uuid "f5407b6a-3e15-5951-adb6-f6a3f6d57b0b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542348}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "beef72c2-a71c-5197-903a-c3c1f057b873" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y),$ traversed along the line segment from $(1,3)$ to $(\\frac{7}{2},4)?$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(1,3)$ to $(\\frac{7}{2},4).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=i+3\\,j$ and $b=\\frac{7}{2}\\,i+4\\,j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =(i+3\\,j)+t[(\\frac{7}{2}\\,i+4\\,j)-(i+3\\,j)] \\\\  & =(i+3\\,j)+t(\\frac{5}{2}\\,i+j) \\\\  & =(1+\\frac{5}{2}t)\\,i+(3+t)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=1+\\frac{5}{2}t$ and $y=3+t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+\\frac{5}{2}t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3+t)j \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\frac{5}{2}}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{29}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((1+\\frac{5}{2}t)+3+t) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4+\\frac{7}{2}t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4+\\frac{7}{2}t)\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{29}}{2}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{29}}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4+\\frac{7}{2}t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542330 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "189f51d6-494f-515a-bf77-59496421b093" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Line Integral Along a Segment in the Cartesian Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11429afc-455f-5c8a-a4df-3cac6e918fb2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{325}{36}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{5}{2}t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5}{3}t-2))\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542286} {:answer/id #uuid "51f0679e-81ac-586f-9a56-1373b928161b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{5}{2}t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5}{3}t-2))\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542299} {:answer/id #uuid "59abc310-6605-56a9-9327-a4ec3f9272b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{13}}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(0-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5}{3}t-2))\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542302} {:answer/id #uuid "5d8df974-825a-516c-b007-9a07e0e2b6fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{13}}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{5}{2}t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2)\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542303} {:answer/id #uuid "f06333fd-751a-5534-ab2f-57975ac048a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{13}}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{5}{2}t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5}{3}t-2))\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542345}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f06333fd-751a-5534-ab2f-57975ac048a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{13}}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{5}{2}t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5}{3}t-2))\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542345} :answer-field/id #uuid "56a67bdf-986e-5011-ab4a-8e0c1bca5fa4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542301}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c47f3d69-bc6d-5af9-a7d6-c2e7d42c806b" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=1+x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y,$ traversed along the line segment from $(-1,2)$ to $(\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{1}{3})?$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(-1,2)$ to $(\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{1}{3}).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=-i+2\\,j$ and $b=\\frac{3}{2}\\,i+\\frac{1}{3}\\,j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =(-i+2\\,j)+t[(\\frac{3}{2}\\,i+\\frac{1}{3}\\,j)-(-i+2\\,j)] \\\\  & =(-i+2\\,j)+t(\\frac{5}{2}\\,i-\\frac{5}{3}\\,j) \\\\  & =(\\frac{5}{2}t-1)\\,i+(-\\frac{5}{3}t+2)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=\\frac{5}{2}t-1$ and $y=-\\frac{5}{3}t+2$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{5}{2}t-1)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\frac{5}{3}t+2)j \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\,i-\\frac{5}{3}\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\frac{5}{2}}^{2}+(- \\frac{5}{3})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\sqrt{13}}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=1+x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =1+(\\frac{5}{2}t-1)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\frac{5}{3}t+2) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5}{3}t-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{5}{2}t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5}{3}t-2))\\cdot \\frac{5\\sqrt{13}}{6}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\sqrt{13}}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{5}{2}t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5}{3}t-2))\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542335 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "189f51d6-494f-515a-bf77-59496421b093" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Line Integral Along a Segment in the Cartesian Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44bb6d25-cb28-57c5-9bd8-8b5a3ea0d8a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{\\frac{3}{2}t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542296} {:answer/id #uuid "69e479cd-9bf0-5481-afeb-58ec5059536d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542308} {:answer/id #uuid "9492d2db-0d16-5473-bbf2-b5753ebbfbd2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{\\frac{5}{2}t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542314} {:answer/id #uuid "9a14126d-fa91-5d78-8230-6c1ec9664546" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{\\frac{5}{2}t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542316} {:answer/id #uuid "f65df61c-e6ae-5911-bed9-45d693eec2b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{\\frac{5}{2}t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542349}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9a14126d-fa91-5d78-8230-6c1ec9664546" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{\\frac{5}{2}t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542316} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9fcc1cb-695e-577d-916f-00bdd3ab93a9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542322}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d489cb8a-37ac-5057-8882-4a94eb183baf" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral of the function $f(x,y)={e}^{x+y},$ traversed along the line segment from $(0,0)$ to $(\\frac{3}{2},1)?$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(0,0)$ to $(\\frac{3}{2},1).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=0$ and $b=\\frac{3}{2}\\,i+j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =0+t\\,(b-0) \\\\  & =t(b) \\\\  & =t(\\frac{3}{2}\\,i+j) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}t\\,i+t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=\\frac{3}{2}t$ and $y=t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{3}{2}t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\frac{3}{2}}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{13}{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)={e}^{x+y}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & ={e}^{\\frac{3}{2}t+t} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\frac{5}{2}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{\\frac{5}{2}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{2}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{\\frac{5}{2}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542336 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "189f51d6-494f-515a-bf77-59496421b093" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Line Integral Along a Segment in the Cartesian Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24fc2385-2b81-5188-bb65-8be9a6b72cf3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{\\frac{17}{6}t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542290} {:answer/id #uuid "31d095c6-9894-5dcd-a47c-fbff4a3ac7aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{145}}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{\\frac{3}{2}t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542293} {:answer/id #uuid "9972107c-2631-5571-a39a-29d7ef026be5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{145}}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{\\frac{4}{3}t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542315} {:answer/id #uuid "a98fbfc4-3c83-5f2b-abab-ba3e760107fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{145}}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{\\frac{17}{6}t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542321} {:answer/id #uuid "d1ee897a-b9ef-5dd3-8d4a-48a525540411" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{145}{36}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{\\frac{17}{6}t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542334}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a98fbfc4-3c83-5f2b-abab-ba3e760107fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{145}}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{\\frac{17}{6}t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542321} :answer-field/id #uuid "dfdb0a89-889d-56b9-a2eb-f7bf05ae6bc7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542340}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d4f4de30-da30-5755-a176-314836964c15" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral of the function $f(x,y)={e}^{x+y},$ traversed along the line segment from $(0,0)$ to $(\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{4}{3})?$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(0,0)$ to $(\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{4}{3}).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=0$ and $b=\\frac{3}{2}\\,i+\\frac{4}{3}\\,j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =0+t\\,(b-0) \\\\  & =t(b) \\\\  & =t(\\frac{3}{2}\\,i+\\frac{4}{3}\\,j) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}t\\,i+\\frac{4}{3}t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=\\frac{3}{2}t$ and $y=\\frac{4}{3}t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{3}{2}t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{4}{3}t)j \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\,i+\\frac{4}{3}\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\frac{3}{2}}^{2}+{\\frac{4}{3}}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{145}{36}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{145}}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)={e}^{x+y}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & ={e}^{\\frac{3}{2}t+\\frac{4}{3}t} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\frac{17}{6}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{\\frac{17}{6}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{145}}{6}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{145}}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{\\frac{17}{6}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Line Integral Along a Segment in the Cartesian Plane"}]}
